<%# WHAT THIS PAGE DOES WHEN IT MOVES — the strip, and the ranking.

    Everything here answers one complaint about the page as it was: the board
    re-ranked correctly and told you nothing about it. Contest::LiveBroadcast
    replaces the whole list on every score, so rows teleported into their new
    order between one blink and the next. Correct, and unreadable — you could
    watch your entry pass three others and never see it happen.

    Live page only. The show page renders the same partial and never loads this,
    because none of it has anything to say about a board that is not moving. %>
<style>
  /* ── THE ROW ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The transform is written by the script (a FLIP: put the row back where it
     used to be, then let it travel to where it now is). All this declares is
     that the row is a thing that moves, so the browser can promote it. */
  .tt-lb-row { will-change: transform; }

  /* ── YOUR ENTRY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Not a colour swap — the row already spends its colours on rank, prize and
     picks. A left-edge post in the CTA colour, which no other state on this
     board uses, so "mine" never has to compete with "first" or "paid". */
  .tt-lb-mine {
    box-shadow: inset 3px 0 0 0 var(--color-cta, #22c55e);
  }

  /* ── MOVEMENT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Written under the rank number, and deliberately absolute: a badge that took
     part in layout would change every row's height the moment the board moved,
     which is the one instant the reader is trying to follow a row with their
     eyes. Empty until the script has TWO renders to compare. */
  .tt-rank-move { transition: opacity .4s ease; }
  .tt-move-up   { color: #4ade80; }
  .tt-move-down { color: #f87171; }

  /* ── THE SCORE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Same idea as the league board's hot score, in this board's idiom: the
     number swells as it changes, then stays lit while the play is still recent
     so a glance answers "what just moved?" without having watched it move. */
  @keyframes ttScorePop {
    0%   { transform: scale(1); }
    35%  { transform: scale(1.45); }
    70%  { transform: scale(.97); }
    100% { transform: scale(1); }
  }
  .tt-score-pop { animation: ttScorePop 620ms cubic-bezier(.2,.9,.3,1.4) both; display: inline-block; }
  [data-role="entry-score"] { transition: color .7s ease, text-shadow .7s ease; }
  .tt-score-hot {
    color: #ffffff;
    text-shadow: 0 0 16px var(--color-cta, #22c55e), 0 0 5px var(--color-cta, #22c55e);
  }

  /* ── THE STRIP ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Sixteen chips, one of which is the game being watched. The focused chip is
     marked in the APP's colour rather than by dimming the other fifteen: those
     fifteen are the reason the strip exists — they are how you notice a game
     worth switching to — and a strip of greyed-out games stops doing that job.
     The lift is small on purpose; the chip is 152px wide and a heavy ring at
     that size eats the scores it is pointing at. */
  .tt-chip { border: 1px solid transparent; }
  .tt-chip:hover { transform: translateY(-1px); }
  .tt-chip-focused {
    border-color: var(--color-cta, #22c55e);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--color-cta, #22c55e), 0 6px 16px -6px rgba(0, 0, 0, .6);
  }
  /* Keyboard parity: the strip is a row of buttons and it has to be usable from
     the keyboard, which means the focus ring must be visible against sixteen
     different team fields. */
  .tt-chip:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-cta, #22c55e);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* The system setting wins. The board still re-ranks, the badge still says
     which way and by how much, the score still lights — they just stop moving,
     which is the distinction the preference actually asks for. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .tt-lb-row    { transition: none !important; transform: none !important; }
    .tt-score-pop { animation: none !important; }
    .tt-chip:hover { transform: none; }
  }
</style>
